        "heading": "4.1 QUALITY COMPARISON OF NON-CONTRASTIVE AND MASK BASED CONTRASTIVE SSL",
        "text": "In order to examine the potential of our non-contrastive SSL method for learning effective speech embeddings, we first compare its performance, for the downstream ASR task, with the SOTA con-\ntrastive SSL method Wav2Vec-2.0 (Baevski et al., 2020). We perform this evaluation by fine-tuning the pre-trained models in two settings. Firstly, with a high-resource setting where large quantities of labeled speech are available. This enables us to compare the effectiveness of speech embeddings learned with contrastive and non-contrastive SSL methods. Secondly, with a low-resource setting where the amount of labeled data is limited. This helps us to compare the usefulness of speech embeddings, learned with the two methods, for improving low resource settings.\nEvaluation with High-Resource Labeled Data. To evaluate the effectiveness of speech embeddings learned with contrastive and non-contrastive SSL methods, we fine-tune the embedding models, pre-trained using W2V-2 and Non-Contrastive methods (discussed in Section ??), with LS-960 dataset. Additionally, we also fine-tune the pre-trained W2V-2 model with Non-Contrastive SSL approach to explore the performance of models when trained iteratively with both approaches (i.e., Iteratively Combined method). Though this approach is computationally inefficient, we investigate it to examine the potential of iterative pre-training when resources are not a constraint or when a pre-trained model is available and we would like to further improve it.\nTo quantify the ASR model performance, we compute word error rate (WER) and also compute character error rate (CER) to examine the performance in terms of label prediction. As presented in Tables 1 and 2, the results for these evaluations show our proposed Non-Contrastive SSL method outperforms the SOTA W2V-2 approach for speech representation learning by achieving up to 6% relative WER improvement on dev/test-clean splits of LibriSpeech dataset.\nAt the same time, our results show the Iteratively Combined SSL approach significantly boosts the performance of downstream ASR task and achieves up to 12% relative WER improvement on dev/test-clean splits. Conclusively, if the priority is to decrease WER rather than training time, Iteratively Combined SSL method is observed to yield the best results.\nEvaluation with Low-Resource Labeled Data. We now evaluate the effectiveness of speech embeddings learned with our non-contrastive SSL method, in comparison to the SOTA contrastive SSL approach (i.e., W2V-2 method), for improving low resource settings. For this evaluation we considered fine-tuning the W2V-2 and Non-Contrastive pre-trained models with LS-100 and LL-10 datasets (discussed in Section 3.2.\nTo quantify the ASR model performance on low resource settings, we compute WER and CER on LibriSpeech dev and test splits. The results for these evaluations are presented in Table 3 and Table 4. Our results show that even on the low resource settings, the speech embeddings learned on unlabeled data with our non-contrastive SSL method are significantly more effective than the ones learned with contrastive SSL method. Specifically, we observe up to 12% relative WER improvement on dev/test-clean sets on both LS-100 and LL-10 datasets. Note that we did not perform HPO for the evaluation with LS-100 and LL-10, instead we used the optimal ones found for LS-960 evaluations. This is reflected in the quality performance compared to that reported in (Baevski et al., 2020). Nevertheless, we do see an improvement in terms of WER/CER performance of Non-Contrastive SSL compared to contrastive W2V-2 method."
